      "source": [
        "**Student Reasoning — Evaluation results**\n",
        "*1. Report your extraction accuracy. Which field was hardest for the model and why?*\n",
        "*2. What did the reliability experiment show about temperature and production systems?*\n",
        "*3. Did your system hallucinate under probing? If yes, how could the prompt (or the system\n",
        "design around it) reduce the risk?*\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Answer:**\n",
        "1. **Accuracy report:**\n",
        "purpose and has_collateral_or_guarantor:100%\n",
        "applicant_name and repayment_months: 66.7%\n",
        "amount_ghs and monthly_profit_ghs: 33.3%\n",
        "\n",
        "**hardest field:** amount_ghs and monthly_profit_ghs:The model got confused by other numbers in the letters, such as mixing up sales with profit, or picking collateral amounts instead of the requested loan amount.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. Result: At both temperature 0.0 and 1.0, the model always gave valid JSON (5/5 runs), but it produced 2 slightly different JSON outputs across the runs (Identical: False).\n",
        "\n",
        "This means that Lower temperature helps keep the JSON valid, but LLMs can still output slight text variations. In production, you need extra code checks to catch these small variations rather than relying only on prompt settings.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Result: No, it did not hallucinate. In Test 2, when given the weather report, it correctly returned null for every field instead of inventing fake applicant details.\n",
        "\n",
        "How to Reduce Risk:\n",
        "Prompting: Clear instructions such as \"use null, do not guess\" stop the model from making up facts when information is missing.\n",
        "System Design: Add an input filter before the prompt to catch and reject non-loan text before it even reaches the extractor."
      ],
