      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "# Section 5 — Reflection\n",
        "\n",
        "*Answer in a few sentences each:*\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Prompting as engineering:** How is iterating on a prompt similar to and different from\n",
        "   iterating on the model hyperparameters you tuned in Lab 3?\n",
        "2. **Trust:** After your Section 4 evaluation, would you trust this system to run unattended?\n",
        "   What single evaluation result most influenced your answer?\n",
        "3. **Cost and scale:** Estimate (from your `response.usage` numbers) the tokens needed to\n",
        "   process 1,000 applications per month. What does that imply for provider choice?\n",
        "4. **Looking back at the course:** You have now used classical ML (Lab 2), trained neural\n",
        "   networks (Lab 3), and used a foundation model via API (Lab 4). For a task like this one,\n",
        "   why does calling an API beat training your own model — and when would it not?\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Answer:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Prompting as Engineering vs. Hyperparameter Tuning\n",
        "How it is similar: Both require an iterative trial-and-error process. Inputs are adjusted- such as changing prompt wording vs. tweaking learning rate or batch size- ran on the model, performance is evaluated against a dataset or gold baseline, and refine based on where errors happen.\n",
        "\n",
        "How it is different: Hyperparameter tuning optimizes numerical parameters through systematic searches like grid or random search using quantitative loss metrics. Prompt engineering uses natural language to guide context, reasoning, and output formats, making it more flexible but less structured to tune.\n",
        "\n",
        "2. No, I can't trust the system to run unattended to.\n",
        "The temperature and reliability experiment where running temperature 0.0 still produced 2 different unique JSON outputs across 5 runs and the lower extraction accuracy on financial numbers like amount_ghs at 33.3%. A system that occasionally shifts output formatting or misinterprets key numbers cannot be left to process financial applications without human supervision.\n",
        "\n",
        "3. Token Estimation (for 1,000 applications/month):\n",
        "\n",
        "Summary Prompt: 300 input + 100 output tokens = 400,000 tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Extract Prompt: 500 input + 100 output tokens = 600,000 tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Brief Prompt: 400 input + 250 output tokens = 650,000 tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "Total Estimate: 1.65 million tokens/month.\n",
        "\n",
        "The implication: Processing millions of tokens monthly makes cost per million tokens and rate limits key factors. Small, fast models like Gemini Flash are far more cost-effective for these structured extraction and summarization tasks than heavy, expensive reasoning models.\n",
        "\n",
        "4. Why calling an API beats training my own model:\n",
        "Foundation models already know how to read and understand complex English right out of the box. Using an API lets you build a working system in just a few minutes using simple prompts. Training your own model from scratch would take weeks of gathering thousands of labeled loan letters, buying expensive graphics cards, and tweaking complex code.\n",
        "\n",
        "When calling an API does not beats training my own model:\n",
        "Privacy & Data Sovereignty: If strict banking laws prohibit sending applicant data to third-party overseas APIs.\n",
        "\n",
        "When the bank operates in areas with unstable internet connectivity and needs local execution.\n"
      ],
      "id": "VrXBYsGhUKz7"
    },
